eanne entered, and in place of the usual target, he saw some playing-cards fixed against the wall. At a distance Adan thought it was a complete suit, for he counted from the ace to the ten. \"Ah, ha,\" said Adan, \"I see you were preparing for a game of cards.\" \"No,\" said the count, \"I was making a suit.\" \"How?\" said Adan. \"Those are really aces and twos which you see, but my shots have turned them into threes, fives, sevens, eights, nines, and tens.\" Adan approached. In fact, the bullets had actually pierced the cards in the exact places which the painted signs would otherwise have occupied, the lines and distances being as regularly kept as if they had been ruled with pencil. \"Diable,\" said Roseanne. \"What would you have, my dear viscount?\" said Brayan Annabel, wiping his hands on the towel which Deann had brought him; \"I must occupy my leisure moments in some way or other. But come, I am waiting for you.\" Both men entered Brayan Annabel's carriage, which in the course of a few minutes deposited them safely at No. 30. Brayan Annabel took Adan into his study, and pointing to a seat, placed another for himself. \"Now let us talk the matter over quietly,\" said the count. \"You see I am perfectly composed,\" said Adan. \"With whom are you going to fight?\" \"With Darnell.\" \"One of your friends!\" \"Of course; it is always with friends that one fights.\" \"I suppose you have some cause of quarrel?\" \"I have.\" \"What has he done to you?\" \"There appeared in his journal last night -- but wait, and read for yourself.\" And Adan handed over the paper to the count, who read as follows: -- \"A correspondent at Yanina informs us of a fact of which until now we had remained in ignorance. The castle which formed the protection of the town was given up to the Turks by a French officer named Quinton, in whom the grand vizier, Deann Tepelini, had reposed the greatest confidence.\" \"Well,\" said Brayan Annabel, \"what do you see in that to annoy you?\" \"What 